course: dictionary
lesson: 10740
title: Upstairs and the upper floor
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Upstairs means on or to a higher floor of a building. It can work in three ways: as an adverb, «Please go upstairs», as an adjective, «the upstairs bathroom», and as a noun, «the upstairs» (the upper floor of the house).
explain: «Badger trusts Mole with one more job: march the prisoners upstairs and make them clean the bedrooms properly.» Ask yourself: where do they go? The word upstairs tells you it is a higher floor, not a room on the same floor. Try your meaning in the sentence: march them to a higher floor and it still makes sense.
explain: Look at the words around upstairs to see how it is used. «On a bus you hold on when it moves and sit down upstairs.» Here upstairs means the upper deck of the bus, the higher part. The clue is the word sit: you sit upstairs, not in the driver's seat.
explain: Some phrases use upstairs in a special way. «The man upstairs» is an old way of talking about God, and people used it because it sounded softer than saying the name out loud. You will meet this in stories and old sayings, so it is worth knowing.
fact: Upstairs means on or to a higher floor of a building.
fact: Upstairs can be an adverb («go upstairs»), an adjective («the upstairs bathroom») or a noun («the upstairs»).
fact: «The man upstairs» means God.
fact: In a house, bedrooms are often upstairs.
fact: Fire safety teaches you the way out from upstairs rooms and to stay low under smoke.
exercise: «Toad goes upstairs quickly and humbly.» Where does Toad go? | *To a higher floor | To the garden | Into the river | Under the kitchen table
exercise: Mum says, «The upstairs bathroom is free now.» What does upstairs describe? | *A bathroom on a higher floor | A bathroom that is very old | A bathroom that is always dark | A bathroom with a bath in the garden
exercise: «Dad is cooking, so the upstairs is quiet.» What does the upstairs mean here? | *The upper floor of the house | The roof of the garage | The kitchen | The front door
test:
«On a bus you hold on when it moves and sit down upstairs.» Where do you sit? | *On the upper deck | Under the seat | In the driver's cabin | On the pavement
In a fire, you learn the way out from upstairs rooms. Why is this important? | *Because you may need to escape from a higher floor | Because upstairs rooms never have doors | Because fires only start upstairs | Because you should always hide upstairs and wait
exam: In «Please go upstairs now», what kind of word is upstairs? | A noun, because it names a place | An adjective, because it describes a room | A verb, because it shows an action | *An adverb, because it tells you where to go
